Ladybird’s tabs.sendMessage() reaches only the extension’s content
scripts in the tab, in every frame or in the one options.frameId names,
and rejects with the no-receiver error when nothing there can receive
it, including a tab or frame that doesn’t exist. Chromium routes it the
same way: TabsHooksDelegate::HandleSendMessage() builds
MessageTarget::ForTab() for the messaging service, which errors when no
frame has a receiver. Gecko’s child ext-tabs.js passes tabId and frameId
to Messenger.sendRuntimeMessage(), and the parent resolves the tab’s
frames. WebKit’s WebExtensionContext::tabsSendMessage() can resolve
successfully with nothing when a tab has no listener; Ladybird follows
Chromium and Gecko there.

The sender a receiver sees carries tab and frameId, filled in by the UI
process from its own view of the sending document. Chromium’s frame ids
are 0 for the top frame and otherwise per-frame ids from
ExtensionApiFrameIdMap::GetFrameId(); Gecko’s getFrameId() in
WebNavigationFrames.sys.mjs uses 0 for the top frame and the browsing
context id for others. Ladybird uses 0 for the top-level navigable and a
positive per-process id for each other navigable, never reused.
documentId isn’t supported, since no Ladybird API hands one out yet.
